    """
    Introduction
    ************


    BTSSM (BTS Site Manager) interface for Robot Framework.

    BTSCommandLineTools are available on the wiki page: `CommandlineTools Wiki <https://twiki.inside.nsn.com/bin/view/SEM/CommandlineTools>`_.

    :Common parameters for all keywords:
        * **alias**   (string)  - Alias is used to distinguish between multiple library settings. Defaults to "default".
        * **ne**      (string)  - BTS TRS IP address. Default value for every keyword is defined in Setup Btssm.
        * **pw**      (string)  - Credentials for BTS Site Manager (TRS). The format is username:password.
                                  Default value for every keyword is defined in Setup Btssm.
        * **ssh**     (string)  - Credentials for the ssh connection to BTS. The format is username:password.
        * **timeout** (integer) - Timeout passed to java application.

        NOTE: Add ne and pw parameters as arguments to other keywords should ne and pw be overridden.
    |
    :Given timeout must be in format '1d 2h 3m 4s 5ms' with following rules:
            *  Time parts having zero value can be ignored (e.g. '3m 4s' is ok)
            *  Format is case and space insensitive
            *  Instead of 'd' it is also possible to use 'day' or 'days'
            *  Instead of 'h' also 'hour' and 'hours' are ok
            *  Instead of 'm' also 'minute', 'minutes', 'min' and 'mins' are ok
            *  Instead of 's' also 'second', 'seconds', 'sec' and 'secs' are ok
            *  Instead of 'ms' also 'millisecond', 'milliseconds' and 'millis' are ok
            *  It is possible to give time only as a float and then it is considered to be seconds
               (e.g. '123', '123.0', '123s', '2min 3s' are all equivalent)


    Package requirements
    ********************


    * Python 2.7.x
    * setuptools >=3.3
    * pip >= 1.5.4
    * ute_installer >= 0.25
    * oracle jre >= 1.8.x


    Multiple instances
    ******************


    This library supports multiple setups where each can be specified by **alias** argument for every keyword.
    This argument can be specified as follow:
    ::

        Setup btssm                                                   alias=btssm1
        Setup btssm               jars_path=/some/custom/path         alias=btssm2


    More examples are available in attached Robot Framework example files.


    Runtime prints
    **************


    Runtime prints (e.g. swupdate process details) are available in the DEBUG mode. These prints are also available
    on the INFO level as a single entry added at the end.


    Installation
    ************


    This section describes how to install **ute_btssm** as single library. For robotlte below steps are not needed.

    Before using the library, make sure to install the CLI Tools first.
        * You can use the command:
            * sudo apt-get install ute-btssm-cli (remember to 'sudo apt-get update' first)
        * Or, for standalone testlines, you can download the tools from https://twiki.int.net.nokia.com/bin/view/SEM/CommandlineTools
            * The recommended location for unpacking the tools is in /opt/btssm_cli_tools/
            * You may choose to install it elsewhere, but you'll need to specify the 'jars_path' argument in the Setup keywords


    Installation for UTE test line
    ------------------------------


    ::

        pip install -U ute_btssm


    Installation for NON UTE test line
    ----------------------------------


    ::

        pip install -U -i http://pypi.ute.inside.nsn.com:25102 pip
        pip install -U -i http://pypi.ute.inside.nsn.com:25102 setuptools
        pip install -U -i http://pypi.ute.inside.nsn.com:25102 ute_installer
        pip install -U -i http://pypi.ute.inside.nsn.com:25102 ute_btssm


    Installation from SVN
    ---------------------

    ::

        pip install svn+https://ca_ute_test:utetest@svne1.access.nsn.com/isource/svnroot/utelibs/ute_btssm/trunk/#egg=ute_btssm


    Keywords
    ********
    """

    def teardown_all_aliases_for_btssm(self):
        """ Calls _out_of_scope method which teardowns all btssms setup by alias """
        self._out_of_scope()

    def _out_of_scope(self):
        for alias_name in self.command_store.aliases:
            self.teardown_btssm(alias=alias_name)

    ROBOT_LIBRARY_SCOPE = 'TEST SUITE'
